<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperial examination</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as examination system</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The imperial examination was the system of competitive examinations, based largely on the Confucian classics, used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to recruit government officials. It developed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> and became central under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> maintained it, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> suspended and restricted it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperial-examination.jpg" alt="Imperial examination" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qiu Ying (仇英) (attributed) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming-Beamtenpr%C3%BCfungen1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
